  "refuted_by": "04 00 walked again with its chain cleared the same way, returning the copy at twice the delay at a setting of `Dly Fb` below 34 or losing it at 95 and up.",
  "what_it_would_change": [],
  "the_round_that_read_the_tail": {
    "the_booking_this_answers": {
      "observable": "How fast 04 00's tail dies after a struck note, with its compressor, overdrive and chorus switched out as they were for this walk, at settings 34, 64 of `Dly Fb`.",
      "predicts": {
        "a loop gain straight in the byte": "3.02 s to fall sixty decibels at 64",
        "the loop-gain readout of the phaser's resonance byte (0120-the-resonance-byte-closes-a-loop)": "2.08 s at 64"
      },
      "divergence": "45 per cent at 64, with the loop taken to be one pass of 300.0 ms and nothing else in it.",
      "sensitivity_here": "The decay stage's own scatter bound on the tail of 04 02, a multi-effect ending in a delay with its loop closed, 12 per cent at the widest octave from 250 Hz up.",
      "margin": 3.8,
      "why_not_separable": null,
      "run": {
        "stage": "decay",
        "type": "04 00",
        "address": "40 03 14",
        "settings": [
          34,
          64
        ],
        "stimulus": "unpitched",
        "takes": 8,
        "minutes": 5
      }
    },
    "what_was_recorded": "Two records, one per setting, each a single wet take against a bypassed take, with the subtraction floor measured from two bypassed takes. Three takes a setting were made and one of each is published, so what follows rests on one take a setting and on the scatter bound each record states for it.",
    "what_came_back": "At 64 the bands the stage could fit read 0.64 and 0.65 s at 250 and 500 Hz and 0.27 and 0.31 s at 2 and 4 kHz; at 34, 0.57 and 0.56 s at 250 and 500 Hz and 0.45 s at 8 kHz. The nearer prediction at 64 is 2.08 s, three times the longest of those, and each record's own scatter bound is at most seventeen per cent. Read as a loop's decay that would miss both readings by far more than the rig resolves, and on the same side.",
    "why_it_is_not_the_quantity_booked": "A loop's integrated tail falls across one pass by exactly the loss of that pass, so a curve read off a loop cannot fall twenty decibels in less than one pass unless the loop loses twenty decibels or more each time round. Every band here was fitted over -5 to -25 dB and the fit lasted between 0.09 and 0.22 s, all of it shorter than the 300 ms pass; the two readings lose 5.95 and 8.64 dB a pass at 64 and 11.45 and 15.05 at 34. So the stage weighed something inside a single pass. The same figure says it a second way: both readings predict the time growing by seventy per cent or more from 34 to 64, and at 250 and 500 Hz it grew by thirteen and sixteen per cent against a scatter of twenty-four and seventeen.",
    "what_the_record_leaves_standing": "Two readings it cannot tell apart. The loop may lose more than twenty decibels a pass at both settings, which is a gain under a tenth -- under both predictions, and no more consistent with one than the other. Or the curve is the struck note's own residual under its onset, integrated from the first sample that stood over the noise and finished before a recirculation arrived; the stage's method integrates from the onset and a record does not say where in the take its fit sat. A reading shorter than both predictions leans towards the shorter of them whatever it is a reading of, so the nearer figure being 2.08 is not evidence for the phaser's table.",
    "why_the_state_does_not_change": "The claim gave up a law in the byte because nothing here weighs a pass, and this run did not weigh one either. It neither separates the two readings nor contradicts what the claim keeps -- that the byte closes a loop and a second pass stands from 95 up -- so the claim stays parked with the same things kept and given up.",
    "what_the_booking_was_priced_on": "A margin of 3.8 over the decay stage's scatter on another type's tail. That scatter is the statistical spread of a line fitted to the integrated curve, and it prices the fit correctly; what it could not say is whether the fit would span the passes a loop gain is read across. On a delay whose pass is longer than the stage's fit lasts, it does not, so the margin was a margin in a quantity the stage did not read here.",
